= Decentralized identity =

== Overview ==
'''Decentralized identity''' is an identity architecture in which subjects or controllers can establish and use identifiers and verifiable claims without depending on one central identity provider for every interaction.

The W3C Decentralized Identifier (DID) standard defines one family of identifiers designed to be decoupled from centralized registries and identity providers. DIDs resolve to DID documents that can describe verification methods and services.

Decentralized identity is broader than DIDs. Systems may combine identifiers, public keys, verifiable credentials, registries, wallets, trust frameworks, and selective-disclosure mechanisms in different ways.

Decentralization does not eliminate trust. Users still rely on issuers for claims, software and registries for resolution, cryptographic assumptions, governance processes, and recovery mechanisms.

== Boundaries ==
* decentralized identity ≠ blockchain identity
* DID ≠ person
* decentralization ≠ absence of issuers, governance, or trust

